Many current advanced interactive generative models, such as GANs and VAEs, exhibit declining performance in high-dimensional settings. Our research focuses on the capabilities of neural tangent kernels, leveraging their potential in high-dimensional settings and the ability to provide certificates of approximation without training. Our work introduces a novel kernel, $k_{nq}$, derived from a non-exchangeable neural tangent kernel. This new kernel is defined by a non-exchangeable neural network with a single hidden layer. In contrast to existing NTKs, which are exchangeable, our proposed kernel leads to significantly faster computational complexity of O(n^2), allowing for efficient learning in high-dimensional settings. Our theoretical analysis demonstrates sufficient representation in high-dimensional settings and sufficient generalization on datasets. Our experiments confirm the efficacy of this model in high-dimensional settings, outperforming previous advanced interactive generative models. Additionally, we have made our code available for public use at https://github.com/kevin18/nft-k. \end{abstract} 

In this paper, we explore the capabilities of neural tangent kernels in high-dimensional settings. Our research demonstrates that these kernels can provide certificates of approximation without training. However, existing kernels are exchangeable and fail to effectively learn in high-dimensional settings. Our solution is a new non-exchangeable neural network with a single hidden layer and a corresponding loss function that maximizes the margin between the model's learned function and data points. This approach leads to efficient computational complexity of O(n^2) and improves representation and generalization on datasets. Our theoretical analysis confirms the efficacy of this model, and our experiments demonstrate improved performance on various benchmark datasets in high-dimensional settings. Our code is available for public use at https://github.com/kevin18/nft-k. \end{abstract}

